# Samba application secrets — template. The real file is .sops.env
# (sops-encrypted dotenv), decrypted on the host into .env by
# decrypt-sops-envs.service and read through `env_file`.
#
# 🚨 QUOTING: a value containing `$` MUST be single-quoted. The decrypt path
# sources these, and Compose interpolates env_file values too (finding #30) —
# verified empirically: single-quoted passes through intact, bare and
# double-quoted get mangled. SMB passwords are a likely place to hit this.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SAMBA_IDAN_PASSWORD — the SMB password for the `idan` user in config.yml.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🚨 Empty or unset is the worst failure shape in this stack, which is why
# samba-init refuses to let the server start on it. The image runs envsubst
# over config.yml; an unset variable becomes "" with no warning; `smbpasswd
# -a -s` then fails; the user is never added to the passdb; and the container
# reports HEALTHY forever, because its healthcheck authenticates anonymously.
# Underneath, the generated [global] carries `map to guest = bad user`, so a
# user missing from the passdb is treated as a GUEST rather than rejected —
# only the share's `valid users` keeps it out.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 24
#
# The username `idan` deliberately MIRRORS the Authentik username. Samba does
# not and cannot bind to Authentik (four independent walls — see compose.yaml's
# header), so this mirroring is the whole mitigation: offboarding is one
# `sopsedit stacks/samba/.sops.env` plus a container restart, because users are
# re-created from config.yml on every start.
SAMBA_IDAN_PASSWORD=changeme_generate_with_openssl_rand_base64_24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SAMBA_INTERFACES — which interfaces smbd binds.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host-specific, which is why it lives here rather than in the git-tracked
# config: the tailnet address is assigned by headscale at runtime, and the
# Proxmox NIC name is not knowable from the repo.
#
# This variable emits BOTH `interfaces = ...` and `bind interfaces only = yes`,
# which is why config.yml does not also set them — a duplicated directive is
# something `testparm` will not flag.
#
# 🚨 `lo` is MANDATORY and samba-init enforces it. The healthcheck dials
# \\localhost, so without loopback the container is permanently unhealthy
# WHILE EVERY REAL CLIENT WORKS PERFECTLY.
#
# Find the NIC name on the services VM with `ip -br link`. On Proxmox with
# virtio it is usually ens18; do not assume it.
#
#   SAMBA_INTERFACES=lo tailscale0 ens18
SAMBA_INTERFACES=changeme_lo_plus_tailscale0_plus_the_real_nic
